\caption(The original one-edge search makes three actual prefix entries across two passes.)
\alttext(Native pass diagram: limit zero enters one vertex and cuts off; limit one enters two vertices and finds the goal.)

> Why the first successful depth is shortest

A depth-limited pass explores directed simple-path prefixes in original outgoing
arc order. A membership flag marks only the current path, and is cleared when an
activation is popped. An examined arc to an active vertex is skipped because it
would close a cycle. Parallel arcs remain separate adjacency entries and can
repeat the same prefix. There is no global visited set across different paths.

Any reachable goal has a simple path: remove a repeated-vertex cycle from a walk
until no vertex repeats. A simple path in an n-vertex graph has at most n−1
edges. A complete depth-limited pass reaches every simple prefix within its
limit unless a goal terminates it early. Thus the first successful limit equals
the minimum edge depth. Among equal-depth solutions, input arc order determines
the first path. This is not a minimum-weight guarantee.

The safe effective limit is \(D=\min(max\_depth,n-1)\). After a failed pass at
D, the loop stops before incrementing it; SIZE_MAX cannot wrap the limit loop.
Unsuccessful requests above n−1 intentionally omit the old redundant passes, so
their historical visit total changes. For requests at or below n−1, the ordinary
path, input-order tie choices and cumulative entry counts are preserved. A
failed smaller budget does not establish that the goal is unreachable.

> Inspect actual visits, not repeated-prefix solves

For a complete binary tree of depth two, the last leaf is visited last in each
relevant depth-first pass. The final pass enters all seven vertices, while
earlier passes enter one and three. The chronological enter records below come
from the same execution that produced the path. Cutoff vertices count as entries
even though their outgoing arcs are not scanned. Success leaves the final path
active; there are no invented leave events after the goal.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/iddfs_tree.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/iterative_deepening_analysis.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto t = dm::algorithms::deepening_tree(2, 2);
    const auto h =
        dm::algorithms::iterative_deepening_dfs_trace(t.n, t.edges, 0, t.goal,
                                                      t.depth);
    std::print("| actual pass limit | entered vertex | path depth |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &e : h.events)
        if (e.kind == dm::algorithms::DeepeningEventKind::enter)
            std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", e.limit, e.vertex, e.depth);
    std::print("Final path {}; cumulative entries {}; final-pass entries {}.\n",
               h.run.result.path, h.run.result.expanded,
               h.run.passes.back().entered);
    std::vector<double> limits, entries, cutoffs, stack;
    for (const auto &q : h.run.passes) {
        limits.push_back(q.limit);
        entries.push_back(q.entered);
        cutoffs.push_back(q.cutoffs);
        stack.push_back(q.maximum_stack);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(limits, entries, "entries", {37, 99, 235}, 2).line(limits, cutoffs,
                                                              "cutoffs",
                                                              {220, 38, 38}, 2).line(limits, stack, "active frames", {22, 163, 74}, 2).title("Binary tree: actual per-pass counts").x_label("depth limit").y_label("count").y_limits(0, 10).save_svg("build/iddfs_tree.svg");
}
```
