\caption(The same graph vertex can be revisited at a shallower path depth; safe depth bounding and complete event admission are explicit.)
\alttext(Four actual passes lead to the path zero three four five; each box reports its actual entry and cutoff totals.)

> What the checks establish

Native sanitizer tests compare all 4096 four-vertex graphs without self-loops at
every source/goal pair with the old recursive-order reference and an independent
BFS shortest-depth oracle. They also check all 512 three-vertex loop graphs,
chronological active paths, every examined original edge id, cycle flags and all
selected pass counts, plus explicit parallel arcs and path-local revisits. A
2001-vertex chain completes with an explicit 2001-frame stack. Exact/one-short
admission, ownership, invalid unused endpoints and safe SIZE_MAX depth controls
are checked. Complete unary/binary/ternary tree controls match the entry formula
for depths zero through seven. All six original search-more tests pass too.
Frozen comparisons preserve 264144 legacy path/count/found results at depths
within n−1, including 2000 ordered loop/parallel-arc controls. A separate Python
recursive oracle matches 1027 cases, 2427 actual passes and 15859 scalar events
exactly. Fresh checks cover all 62 definitions, 12 compiled public examples and
four representative clangd hovers.

The examples establish minimum edge depth and actual repeat-work behavior for
this finite directed search. They do not establish an optimal weighted path,
constant overhead on arbitrary graphs, total O(d) memory, or a runtime crossover
against BFS. No unrelated dataset is needed for these graph-mechanism controls.

The primary source is [Richard E. Korf (1985), Depth-first iterative-deepening:
An optimal admissible tree
search](https://doi.org/10.1016/0004-3702(85)90084-0), Artificial Intelligence
27(1), 97–109. Its exponential-tree setting is essential to the constant-factor
claim. This implementation is the unweighted directed finite-graph variant with
current-path cycle exclusion, explicit frames and the simple-path bound; it is
not heuristic IDA*. The original repeat-level proof on page 101 was checked.
Counts here include the root and limit-zero pass, and the plotted ratio divides
by an entire final pass; these conventions differ from normalizing asymptotic
work by only the deepest level.
